/-!
# The odd-core or component escape in the actual Thompson image

In all five adequate B.5.6 alternatives, the two-residual lies in the layer
times the full three-core. The direct-factor calculation retains the original
normal inclusions. In the outer A₆–C₃ configuration the remaining quotient
is cyclic and generated by images of elementary two-subgroups, hence a
two-group; no order is assumed for the specified outer generator.

The original Sylow subgroup supplies the rest of the Thompson image. Thus
an escaping Thompson subgroup forces the full preimage of its three-core
or an ordinary component to escape M. The component also remains an ordinary
component in the ambient centralizer quotient, by relative subnormality.
NonFFSourceDoubledBinaryThompsonEscape applies this to the original doubled
orbit. Excluding these two branches remains with qt-d-1222-nonff-doubled.
Source: Aschbacher–Smith, proof of Proposition 7.7.3, printed p.707,
refs/quasithin.pdf; B.5.6, printed p.105.
-/

public noncomputable section
open Subgroup Representation Representation.BinaryOffender Theory.GroupTheory
open scoped IsMulCommutative MatrixGroups
namespace CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter7.NormalizedSource.DoubledBinaryThompsonEscape
